9.1 — View Boot Logs
￼
bash
# Kernel ring buffer (hardware detection, module loading)
dmesg | less
dmesg | grep -i error

# Current boot journal
journalctl -b

# Previous boot journal (if persistent journal is configured)
journalctl -b -1

# Only kernel messages
journalctl -k

# Follow live
journalctl -f
9.2 — Enable Persistent Journal
By default, journal logs may not survive reboot:
scribd
+1

￼
bash
# Create the persistent directory
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/journal

# Restart journald
sudo systemctl restart systemd-journald

# Verify you can see previous boots
journalctl --list-boots
9.3 — Analyze Boot Performance
￼
bash
# Total boot time breakdown
systemd-analyze

# Blame: which services took the longest
systemd-analyze blame

# Generate a visual SVG timeline
systemd-analyze plot > boot-timeline.svg
